{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_000000000000000000000001","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Running Client Onboarding as a Consultancy\n\nOnboarding is the first real test of a consultancy's promise. Before any analysis is delivered, the client forms a view of whether the firm is organised, whether it listens, and whether it will be worth the fee. A sloppy start is hard to recover from, because the client's early impressions set the terms for everything that follows. A consultancy should treat onboarding as a defined process with owners, deadlines, and checkpoints, not as an administrative chore that happens before the real work begins.\n\nThe process starts before the contract is signed. The sales team should hand over a short written brief that records what the client said they need, why now, who decides, and what they have already tried. Promises made during the sale should be listed explicitly, so the delivery team does not inherit commitments nobody remembers making. Vague assurances such as \"we can move quickly\" are a common source of later friction. A good handover replaces them with dates and named deliverables.\n\nNext comes the kickoff. It should be short, structured, and built around decisions rather than introductions. The consultancy should confirm the scope, the success measures, the governance (who approves what, and how often the steering group meets), and the working rhythm. The firm should also"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7191,"cache_read_input_tokens":8072,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7191},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"00000000-0000-4000-8000-00000000